<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="51997" href="schedule/2018/spring/CMN/529/51997.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2018" href="schedule/2018.xml">2018</calendarYear><term id="120181" href="schedule/2018/spring.xml">Spring 2018</term><subject id="CMN" href="schedule/2018/spring/CMN.xml">Communication</subject><course id="529" href="schedule/2018/spring/CMN/529.xml">Seminar Communication Theory</course></parents><sectionNumber>WB </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Qualitative Field Methods</sectionTitle><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>This is an applied course examining the practice of qualitative field methods, with an emphasis on the study of knowledge intensive contexts. Students will approach this subject by: 1) reviewing exemplars of grounded qualitative inquiry, 2) surveying methods texts, and 3) gaining their own experiences in the field. Enrolled students should come prepared with an idea of a qualitative field project they would like to perform throughout the semester.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2018-01-16Z</startDate><endDate>2018-05-02Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>02:00 PM</start><end>04:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>R      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>4007</roomNumber><buildingName>Lincoln Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Barley" firstName="W">Barley, W</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>